<BaseLayout title="Storage and Handling of Lyophilized Peptides" description="How temperature, moisture and freeze–thaw cycles degrade lyophilized peptides, and the handling practices that preserve them." canonical="/knowledge/peptide-storage-handling/">
  <div class="wrap-narrow">
    <p class="breadcrumb"><a href="/">Home</a> › <a href="/knowledge/">Knowledge</a> › Storage &amp; handling</p>
    <h1>Storage and handling of lyophilized peptides</h1>
    <p class="lead muted">Most peptide degradation happens after the vial leaves the factory. These are the variables that matter.</p>

    <p class="small muted" style="margin-bottom:18px;">Part of the <a href="/knowledge/peptide-handling-laboratory-practice/">handling &amp; laboratory practice pillar</a> — the end-to-end chain from receipt to result.</p>
    <h2>Temperature</h2>
    <p>Lyophilized peptides are typically stored at −20 °C for long-term stability, or 2–8 °C for shorter periods. Repeated temperature cycling is worse than a stable cold chain: condensation forms each time a cold vial meets warm air.</p>

    <h2>Moisture</h2>
    <p>Lyophilized cakes are hygroscopic. Open vials in a humid room and the powder absorbs water, which accelerates hydrolysis and aggregation. Equilibrate sealed vials to room temperature before opening, and work quickly. The weighing implications — drift, static and transfer loss — are set out in <a href="/knowledge/peptide-weighing-practice/">weighing small peptide quantities</a>.</p>

    <h2>Light</h2>
    <p>Many aromatic residues are photosensitive. Amber vials and dark storage protect the material; a windowsill does not. Which residues absorb, why a solution is more exposed than a cake, and what a photostability study can prove is covered in <a href="/knowledge/peptide-photostability/">light exposure and peptide stability</a>.</p>

    <h2>Freeze–thaw</h2>
    <p>If you reconstitute, aliquot before freezing. Each freeze–thaw cycle can cause precipitation and loss of activity. Single-use aliquots are the standard practice.</p>

    <h2>Recommended workflow</h2>
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th>Step</th><th>Practice</th><th>Reason</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Receipt</td><td>Store at −20 °C immediately</td><td>Minimise time at ambient</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Before opening</td><td>Equilibrate sealed vial to room temperature</td><td>Prevent condensation</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Reconstitution</td><td>Use appropriate laboratory-grade solvent; swirl, don't vortex</td><td>Preserve structure</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Aliquoting</td><td>Split into single-use volumes</td><td>Avoid repeat freeze–thaw</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Storage of solution</td><td>2–8 °C short term, −20 °C long term</td><td>Slow hydrolysis</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>

    <div class="ruo">For laboratory research use only.</div>

    <h2>Editor's take</h2>
    <p>The vial usually degrades in the lab, not in transit. Condensation on a cold vial and a vortexed stock explain more lost experiments than a bad batch ever will.</p>

